1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,135
Welcome back. In this video,

2
00:00:03,135 --> 00:00:05,340
we're going to discuss
project closing.

3
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:06,840
We'll answer the questions,

4
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:08,775
what does project closing mean,

5
00:00:08,775 --> 00:00:11,805
why is it important, and
when does it occur?

6
00:00:11,805 --> 00:00:14,070
Project closing consists of

7
00:00:14,070 --> 00:00:16,795
the process performed to
formally complete the project,

8
00:00:16,795 --> 00:00:19,860
the current phase, and
contractual obligations.

9
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:22,340
The first thing you should
know is that completing

10
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:26,240
a project is not the same
thing as closing a project.

11
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:28,160
Just because a project is done

12
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:29,725
doesn't mean it's closed.

13
00:00:29,725 --> 00:00:31,310
In a restaurant, just

14
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:33,230
because you've ordered
your meal and finished it,

15
00:00:33,230 --> 00:00:35,615
doesn't mean your dining
experience is over.

16
00:00:35,615 --> 00:00:37,585
You have to pay the
bill before you leave.

17
00:00:37,585 --> 00:00:39,925
It's the same idea with projects.

18
00:00:39,925 --> 00:00:43,075
What does it actually take
for a project to be closed?

19
00:00:43,075 --> 00:00:47,035
There are three criteria that
make up a project closing.

20
00:00:47,035 --> 00:00:50,030
You'll want to assure
all work is done.

21
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:51,560
Ensure that the agreed upon

22
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:53,225
project management processes are

23
00:00:53,225 --> 00:00:55,790
executed and get formal
recognition from

24
00:00:55,790 --> 00:00:58,675
stakeholders that
the project is done.

25
00:00:58,675 --> 00:01:01,605
First, you must assure
all work is done.

26
00:01:01,605 --> 00:01:02,900
There's a chance one of

27
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:04,684
your tasks may have
been overlooked.

28
00:01:04,684 --> 00:01:06,950
Let's say there was a
change made that called for

29
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:09,395
reprioritizing work
during the project.

30
00:01:09,395 --> 00:01:11,390
For instance, when your team

31
00:01:11,390 --> 00:01:13,100
completed user acceptance testing

32
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:14,390
for Project Plant Pals,

33
00:01:14,390 --> 00:01:16,150
everything was finished
and wrapped up.

34
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:18,590
But months later, a
customer searches for

35
00:01:18,590 --> 00:01:20,420
allergy information
about the plants

36
00:01:20,420 --> 00:01:22,010
on Office Green's site,

37
00:01:22,010 --> 00:01:24,670
but that information is
nowhere to be found.

38
00:01:24,670 --> 00:01:27,395
If you did a review before
wrapping the project,

39
00:01:27,395 --> 00:01:29,195
you might have found
that this task,

40
00:01:29,195 --> 00:01:31,220
creating allergy
documentation, was

41
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:33,670
overlooked and therefore
not completed.

42
00:01:33,670 --> 00:01:35,600
In doing a review of the project,

43
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:38,000
you double- and triple-check
that all work is

44
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,450
done to avoid having to
revisit the project later on.

45
00:01:41,450 --> 00:01:43,850
Next, you must ensure
that all agreed

46
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:46,745
upon project management
processes are executed.

47
00:01:46,745 --> 00:01:49,840
Sometimes, managerial
tasks get overlooked.

48
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:51,935
If the task itself is finished,

49
00:01:51,935 --> 00:01:54,020
the procedural or
administrative work that

50
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:56,480
needs to take place afterwards
might slip your mind.

51
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:57,890
An example might be

52
00:01:57,890 --> 00:02:00,110
getting contracts
signed and processed.

53
00:02:00,110 --> 00:02:01,550
It might have been months since

54
00:02:01,550 --> 00:02:02,945
the project was completed,

55
00:02:02,945 --> 00:02:04,670
but when you revisit the contract

56
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:05,795
with your plant provider,

57
00:02:05,795 --> 00:02:07,415
you realize that neither of you

58
00:02:07,415 --> 00:02:09,410
ended up actually signing it.

59
00:02:09,410 --> 00:02:11,390
This is a crucial misstep that

60
00:02:11,390 --> 00:02:13,430
leaves both parties
vulnerable, and

61
00:02:13,430 --> 00:02:15,380
it's now taking place way after

62
00:02:15,380 --> 00:02:17,420
the new services official launch.

63
00:02:17,420 --> 00:02:20,030
Finally, you'll need
formal recognition and

64
00:02:20,030 --> 00:02:21,380
agreement that the project is

65
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:23,480
complete by key stakeholders.

66
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:25,685
If you don't have
formal approval from

67
00:02:25,685 --> 00:02:27,935
all stakeholders that
a project is over,

68
00:02:27,935 --> 00:02:30,200
certain stakeholders
may still request

69
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:31,805
adjustments to the project

70
00:02:31,805 --> 00:02:33,920
because they'll think
it's still active.

71
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:36,955
This may affect various
members of your team.

72
00:02:36,955 --> 00:02:38,190
For instance, if

73
00:02:38,190 --> 00:02:40,250
Office Green's
contracted web developers

74
00:02:40,250 --> 00:02:42,680
believe that the
project is still active,

75
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:44,180
they may still be dedicating

76
00:02:44,180 --> 00:02:47,345
time and even billing hours
of work on this project,

77
00:02:47,345 --> 00:02:50,105
which means wasted money
for Office Green.

78
00:02:50,105 --> 00:02:51,950
In the same way that all of

79
00:02:51,950 --> 00:02:53,030
the previous phases of

80
00:02:53,030 --> 00:02:55,610
the process, like
initiation, planning,

81
00:02:55,610 --> 00:02:59,420
executing, monitoring, and
controlling serve a purpose,

82
00:02:59,420 --> 00:03:02,815
closing serves its own,
equally important purpose.

83
00:03:02,815 --> 00:03:05,270
Closing a project is
important because it

84
00:03:05,270 --> 00:03:08,515
ensures that nothing has
fallen through the cracks.

85
00:03:08,515 --> 00:03:11,700
If a project isn't closed,
your team's efforts,

86
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:15,080
time, and credibility may
be negatively impacted.

87
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:17,450
To avoid negative
impacts to your team,

88
00:03:17,450 --> 00:03:19,130
there are a couple of
different types of

89
00:03:19,130 --> 00:03:22,660
projects that you'll want
to know about and avoid.

90
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:25,185
These are the
never-ending project

91
00:03:25,185 --> 00:03:26,805
and the abandoned project.

92
00:03:26,805 --> 00:03:30,650
The never-ending project exists
when, for whatever reason,

93
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:34,315
the project deliverables and
tasks cannot be completed.

94
00:03:34,315 --> 00:03:36,110
This may occur when tasks are

95
00:03:36,110 --> 00:03:38,240
delegated to team
members who don't have

96
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:40,640
the skills necessary
to complete the tasks

97
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:43,420
or when deadlines aren't
properly communicated,

98
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:45,920
maybe when user
acceptance testing yields

99
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:48,290
too many non-launch
blocking bugs,

100
00:03:48,290 --> 00:03:50,285
or when your client
is unsatisfied,

101
00:03:50,285 --> 00:03:52,120
despite meeting
their requirements.

102
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:53,450
Pay special attention to

103
00:03:53,450 --> 00:03:55,370
protecting the scope
of your project,

104
00:03:55,370 --> 00:03:56,810
and you're much more likely to

105
00:03:56,810 --> 00:03:58,525
close a project successfully.

106
00:03:58,525 --> 00:04:00,740
If it feels like the
customer wants much more

107
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:03,335
from you than this project
is slated to deliver,

108
00:04:03,335 --> 00:04:05,540
maybe the best option
is to commit to

109
00:04:05,540 --> 00:04:08,120
a follow-up project and
close the current one.

110
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:10,130
The abandoned project exists when

111
00:04:10,130 --> 00:04:13,270
inadequate handoff of the
project deliverables occurs.

112
00:04:13,270 --> 00:04:14,930
Basically, the final deliverable

113
00:04:14,930 --> 00:04:16,790
never makes it to your customer.

114
00:04:16,790 --> 00:04:19,160
It wouldn't make sense
to build a product and

115
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:21,470
then not be able to
market it or sell it.

116
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:23,000
Making plans to ensure

117
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,700
an adequate handoff or
transition deliverables is

118
00:04:25,700 --> 00:04:27,500
crucial to ensure customers are

119
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:30,070
happy and projects
are properly closed.

120
00:04:30,070 --> 00:04:31,985
In summary, you'll want to do

121
00:04:31,985 --> 00:04:33,860
everything you can
to properly close

122
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:35,690
a project because
it may leave you

123
00:04:35,690 --> 00:04:37,595
on the hook for
incomplete contracts,

124
00:04:37,595 --> 00:04:41,065
incomplete scope, or
non-compliant practices.

125
00:04:41,065 --> 00:04:42,515
In the next video,

126
00:04:42,515 --> 00:04:44,990
we'll discuss the necessary
steps included in

127
00:04:44,990 --> 00:04:46,895
a comprehensive closing process

128
00:04:46,895 --> 00:04:49,650
for clients and stakeholders.
